#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="Report: which In Progress issues have landed, and which are abandoned claims (reads get_issue payloads on stdin) — CLOUD-469"
#
# CLOUD-469. `landed-check` computes the right predicate and NOTHING CALLS IT.
# Verified 2026-08-20: `grep -rn landed-check` over mise.toml, mise-tasks/,
# .github/ and hk.pkl returns prose only — six task headers cite the predicate,
# no caller invokes it. A gate with no caller is a sensor with no gate, the shape
# non-negotiable 2 refuses, and the cost is a column that only grows: 39 rows In
# Progress for one assignee on 2026-08-12, still 32 on 2026-08-20 with 20 of them
# sitting behind git.
#
# The asymmetry is why it accumulates by construction. INTO In Progress is
# automated — the tracker moves a row when a commit mentions it, and only ever
# forward. OUT of In Progress is manual, and happens only if the session that
# pulled the issue also lands it AND remembers. A session that is interrupted,
# reclaimed or simply ends leaves the row behind and nothing revisits it.
#
# TWO VERDICTS, because the column is stale in two different ways and they need
# different evidence:
#
#   landed-unswept     In Progress and its ref is on `main`. DELEGATED to
#                      `landed-check`, never recomputed here — see below.
#   claimed-abandoned  In Progress and no ref on `main` and no PR attachment and
#                      no remote branch for its `gitBranchName` and idle longer
#                      than WIP_MAX_IDLE_DAYS.
#
# THE DELEGATION IS THE DESIGN, not politeness. CLOUD-469 §2: "If this grows its
# own opinion about what 'landed' means, there are two authorities for one fact
# and only the newer one decides — the CLOUD-351 shape. It calls `landed-check`
# or it is wrong." So the landed half is a pipe into that task and a read of its
# exit code and its lines. Re-implementing `git log --grep` here would be a
# second authority that drifts silently the first time either is edited.
#
# WHY THE ABANDONED HALF NEEDS A BOUND, and why the bound is the whole gate. An
# abandoned claim has nothing on `main` to be reachable, so `landed-check` is
# structurally blind to it and always will be. Measured on CLOUD-113: a session
# claimed it, built it, committed `68292fe` locally, and died to a quota
# exhaustion before its first push. No branch, no PR, no attachment — the row sat
# In Progress, assigned, with a plausible `startedAt`, indistinguishable from live
# work. A later session pulled it and rebuilt it from scratch: two independent
# implementations of one issue, CLOUD-49's shape, the duplicate `claim-check` and
# `issue-guard` exist to prevent and could not see because there was never a PR.
#
# But a session that pulled an issue TEN MINUTES AGO also has no ref, no PR and
# no branch. Without a staleness bound this verdict would name live work as dead,
# which is worse than the stale column it is fixing — measured on this very board
# 2026-08-20, where five of the twelve non-landed rows were live and a bound-free
# predicate would have stranded all five. So `updatedAt` carries the bound. It is
# not a heartbeat and does not pretend to be one; it is the only rolling signal a
# board row has, and what it bounds is how long we keep believing a claimant that
# has stopped producing evidence — the framing `mem:workflow/landing-loop` records
# for the lease's rolling TTL, one signal shallower.
#
# WHAT THE DELEGATED HALF IS WORTH, measured on the first run 2026-08-20, and why
# its label says CANDIDATES. `landed-check` matches a bare mention of the id in
# `main`'s log, and a commit can cite an issue as prior art, in a list of past
# failures, or in a deferral. Over the 32-row column it named 19; adjudicated
# against PR state, 2 were closed by a merged PR and 2 — CLOUD-271 and CLOUD-401,
# both matched on citations — had their live work in an OPEN pull request.
#
# The imprecision is inherited, not introduced: this task delegates because
# CLOUD-469 §2 settles that there is one authority for "landed", and sharpening
# the predicate here would make a second. CLOUD-804 carries that fix. What is
# this task's to get right is not presenting a grep as a conclusion — hence the
# CANDIDATES wording, which points the reader at the gate that actually decides
# a move. Reporting a number a reader cannot act on in bulk, without saying so,
# is how CLOUD-480 sat wrong for 4.5 hours.
#
# WHAT `updatedAt` CANNOT SEE, measured while first running this 2026-08-20 and
# stated here because a reader will otherwise trust the bound further than it
# goes. `updatedAt` advances on ANY write to the row — a label, a relation, a
# comment — not only on evidence that the claim is alive. Adding `relatedTo`
# links to CLOUD-469 in that same session reset CLOUD-124's timestamp from four
# days stale to minutes old, and CLOUD-124 is one of the rows the verdict exists
# to name. So a bulk edit launders a dead claim, and the drain then reports a
# column cleaner than it is.
#
# It is still the right signal, because the alternatives are worse: `startedAt`
# never moves and would call every long-running claim dead, and there is no
# per-row heartbeat to read. What follows is that a ZERO here is weak evidence
# where a non-zero is strong — this verdict can only ever under-report, never
# over-report, which is the safe direction for a report that could otherwise
# strand live work. Narrowing it to evidence-bearing writes needs a field the
# tracker does not expose; that is a row of its own, not a knob.
#
# WHY THIS REPORTS AND NEVER MOVES (CLOUD-469 §2). A state change is a decision.
# An automatic drain would rebuild CLOUD-186's defect from the other side: that
# automation moves a row on a mere mention, and the damage was precisely that
# nobody chose it. Naming the rows is the deliverable; `board-move-guard` still
# adjudicates every actual move.
#
# NOT IN THE hk GATE, the split `branch-age-check` and `lock-complete` already
# made: the answer is a property of the WORLD (the remote's refs, the tracker's
# column), not of the commit being judged. On the landing path it would fail
# whichever PR happened to be in flight over somebody else's stale row — a
# finding that PR did not cause and cannot fix. It runs by hand and on the
# branch-hygiene clock.
#
# Both readings are injectable so the suite runs offline and with no `gh`:
#   WIP_DRAIN_REFS   file of remote branch names, one a line
#   WIP_DRAIN_TODAY  today as YYYY-MM-DD
#
# Exit 0 clean / 1 the column is stale / 2 could-not-look — never a silent pass.
#
# MUTATION COVERAGE (CLOUD-418). `<slug>|<sed script>|<case name>`. Each row
# removes one conjunct, and the named case is the one that stops discriminating.
#MUTANT abandoned-ignores-landed|s@landed_json=.*@landed_json="[]"@|a landed row is not ALSO reported as abandoned — the verdicts are exclusive
#MUTANT abandoned-ignores-pr|s@\[ "$has_pr" = true \] && continue@false \&\& continue@|a claim carrying a PR attachment is not abandoned
#MUTANT abandoned-ignores-branch|s@in_refs "$branch" && continue@false \&\& continue@|a claim with a live remote branch is not abandoned
#MUTANT abandoned-ignores-idle|s@\[ "$age" -gt "$max_idle" \] .*@true@|a claim touched today is not abandoned
set -uo pipefail

max_idle="${WIP_MAX_IDLE_DAYS:-2}"

cannot_look() {
	echo "::error:: in-progress-drain: $1" >&2
	exit 2
}

here="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"

# --- the payload --------------------------------------------------------------
if ! payload=$(cat) || [ -z "${payload//[[:space:]]/}" ]; then
	cannot_look "stdin is empty; expected get_issue payloads"
fi
if ! issues=$(jq -sc 'if length == 1 and (.[0] | type == "array") then .[0] else . end' <<<"$payload" 2>/dev/null) ||
	! jq -e 'all(.[]; has("id") and has("status"))' <<<"$issues" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
	cannot_look "stdin is not a set of get_issue payloads (need id and status per issue)"
fi

today="${WIP_DRAIN_TODAY:-$(date -u +%Y-%m-%d)}"

# --- the remote's branch names ------------------------------------------------
if [ -n "${WIP_DRAIN_REFS:-}" ]; then
	refs=$(cat "$WIP_DRAIN_REFS" 2>/dev/null) ||
		cannot_look "cannot read WIP_DRAIN_REFS ($WIP_DRAIN_REFS)"
else
	refs=$(git ls-remote --heads origin 2>/dev/null | sed 's@.*refs/heads/@@') ||
		cannot_look "cannot list the remote's branches"
	[ -n "$refs" ] ||
		cannot_look "the remote reports no branches at all, which cannot be true of a repository with a trunk"
fi

# --- helpers ------------------------------------------------------------------
in_refs() {
	[ -n "$1" ] && grep -qxF "$1" <<<"$refs"
}
# Days-since-epoch by the civil-calendar formula, integer arithmetic only.
# `mktime` is a gawk extension mawk does not carry — the portability trap
# `awk-regex-check` exists for. Lifted from `branch-age-check`, which is the
# origin; a third copy should become a shared helper rather than a third lift.
daynum() {
	awk -v iso="$1" '
		function days(y, m, d,   era, yoe, doy, doe) {
			if (m <= 2) y -= 1
			era = int((y >= 0 ? y : y - 399) / 400)
			yoe = y - era * 400
			doy = int((153 * (m + (m > 2 ? -3 : 9)) + 2) / 5) + d - 1
			doe = yoe * 365 + int(yoe / 4) - int(yoe / 100) + doy
			return era * 146097 + doe - 719468
		}
		BEGIN {
			s = substr(iso, 1, 10)
			# Shape first, then split: `not-a-date` splits into three parts on
			# the hyphen exactly as `2026-08-11` does, and the arithmetic would
			# then answer a number instead of admitting it cannot read the value.
			if (s !~ /^[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]$/) { print "NaN"; exit }
			split(s, p, "-")
			print days(p[1] + 0, p[2] + 0, p[3] + 0)
		}'
}

now=$(daynum "$today")
[ "$now" != NaN ] || cannot_look "cannot read today's date ('$today')"
# --- the landed half, delegated -----------------------------------------------
#
# `landed-check` names each landed row on stderr as "  <id>  In Progress -> In
# Review" and exits 1; exit 0 is a clean board and exit 2 is could-not-look. Its
# 2 is ours: if it cannot resolve `main`, neither half of this report is safe,
# and the abandoned verdict in particular would call every landed row abandoned.
# The merged-PR evidence is the caller's to supply and this task is a caller
# (CLOUD-804). `DRAIN_MERGED_PRS` names a prepared file; with none, this gathers
# it via `merged-pr-keys` rather than refusing, which is what makes the drain
# runnable in a fresh clone with no ad-hoc scripting (CLOUD-814 — the gap left by
# shipping the consumer without a producer).
#
# GATHERING HERE IS NOT A CREDENTIAL LEAKING INTO A BOARD GATE. `landed-check` is
# one of the six gh-free payload gates and stays untouched; this is the fetch
# side of agents-fetch-gates-decide, and the fetch side is where a credential
# belongs. The suite always injects `DRAIN_MERGED_PRS`, so no case reaches the
# branch below and the suite stays offline.
#
# A failed gather is NOT swallowed into a short sweep: `merged-pr-keys` exits 2
# on a truncated or empty forge answer, and that becomes this task's 2.
drain_evidence=()
if [ -n "${DRAIN_MERGED_PRS:-}" ]; then
	drain_evidence=(--merged-prs "$DRAIN_MERGED_PRS")
else
	gathered="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/merged-pr-keys.$$"
	if ! "$here/merged-pr-keys" >"$gathered" 2>/dev/null; then
		rm -f "$gathered"
		cannot_look "no DRAIN_MERGED_PRS was set and \`merged-pr-keys\` could not gather the evidence itself. Run it directly to see why, or set DRAIN_MERGED_PRS to a prepared file."
	fi
	trap 'rm -f "$gathered"' EXIT
	drain_evidence=(--merged-prs "$gathered")
fi
landed_report=$("$here/landed-check" "${drain_evidence[@]+"${drain_evidence[@]}"}" <<<"$issues" 2>&1)
landed_status=$?
case "$landed_status" in
0 | 1) ;;
*) cannot_look "landed-check could not judge the payload, so landedness is unknown and every row would read as abandoned. Its report: ${landed_report:-(none)}" ;;
esac
landed_ids=$(printf '%s\n' "$landed_report" | sed -n 's@^  \([A-Z][A-Z0-9]*-[0-9][0-9]*\)  In Progress -> In Review$@\1@p' | sort -u)
landed_json=$(jq -Rsc 'split("\n") | map(select(. != ""))' <<<"$landed_ids")

# CLOUD-783's lesson, applied at construction rather than after a quiet sweep: a
# projected-away key must be a NAMED refusal, never a rule that silently scans
# nothing.
#
# DEMANDED OF THE ROWS WHOSE VERDICT STILL DEPENDS ON IT, never of the column.
# Two conjuncts are already decidable here — landedness, which `landed-check`
# answered from `id` and `status`, and the idle bound, which reads `updatedAt` —
# and a row either resolves on one of them or it does not. Demanding three more
# keys to re-answer a resolved row refuses a sweep that has no question about it:
# the over-refusal twin of the silent scan.
#
# It is also the whole cost argument. `list_issues` projects `id`, `status`,
# `gitBranchName` and `updatedAt` but CANNOT project `attachments`, so
# `get_issue` — which takes no field selection and drags every byte of the body
# — is the only source of the one key left. Ordering the conjuncts by what is
# projectable means the expensive fetch is paid only for rows that are landed-
# free AND past the idle bound. Measured on this board 2026-08-20: 32 In
# Progress, 19 resolved landed, 6 more inside the bound, so 7 full fetches
# instead of 32.
candidates=$(jq -c --argjson landed "$landed_json" --argjson now "$now" --argjson max "$max_idle" '
	[ .[]
	  | select(.status == "In Progress")
	  | select(.id as $i | $landed | index($i) | not)
	  | .id ]' <<<"$issues")

# The idle bound needs `updatedAt`, so that key is demanded of every unresolved
# row — it is projectable, so this costs nothing to satisfy.
missing=$(jq -r --argjson c "$candidates" '[.[] | select(.id as $i | $c | index($i)) | select(has("updatedAt") | not) | .id] | join(" ")' <<<"$issues")
[ -z "$missing" ] ||
	cannot_look "no \`updatedAt\` on unresolved In Progress issue(s): $missing. The idle bound reads that key, so a payload without it cannot answer — \`list_issues\` projects it."

stale=""
unreadable=""
while IFS=$'\t' read -r id updated; do
	[ -n "$id" ] || continue
	d=$(daynum "$updated")
	if [ "$d" = NaN ]; then
		unreadable="$unreadable $id"
		continue
	fi
	age=$((now - d))
	[ "$age" -gt "$max_idle" ] || continue
	stale="$stale $id"
done < <(jq -r --argjson c "$candidates" '.[] | select(.id as $i | $c | index($i)) | [.id, (.updatedAt // "")] | @tsv' <<<"$issues" | sort -u)

stale_json=$(jq -Rsc '[splits("[[:space:]]+")] | map(select(. != ""))' <<<"$stale")

# Only now is the un-projectable key worth demanding, and only of what is left.
for key in attachments gitBranchName; do
	missing=$(jq -r --arg k "$key" --argjson st "$stale_json" '[.[] | select(.id as $i | $st | index($i)) | select(has($k) | not) | .id] | join(" ")' <<<"$issues")
	[ -n "$missing" ] || continue
	cannot_look "no \`$key\` on stale In Progress issue(s): $missing. The claimed-abandoned verdict reads that key, so a payload without it cannot answer — re-fetch with \`get_issue\` (\`list_issues\` cannot project \`attachments\`)."
done

# --- the abandoned half -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Only the stale, landed-free rows reach here, and only the two un-projectable
# conjuncts are left to decide.
abandoned=""
while IFS=$'\t' read -r id branch has_pr; do
	[ -n "$id" ] || continue
	[ "$has_pr" = true ] && continue
	in_refs "$branch" && continue
	abandoned="$abandoned $id"
done < <(jq -r --argjson st "$stale_json" '
	.[]
	| select(.id as $i | $st | index($i))
	| [ .id,
	    (.gitBranchName // ""),
	    ([ .attachments[]? | .url // "" ] | any(test("/pull/[0-9]+"))) ]
	| @tsv' <<<"$issues" | sort -u)

# --- the report ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Pointer-only per non-negotiable rule 4: keys, verdict labels and counts. A body
# never reaches this output, and the suite asserts that rather than trusting it.
fail=0
if [ -n "$landed_ids" ]; then
	fail=1
	echo "::error:: landed-unswept — In Progress while their work is on main; landed is In Review (AGENTS.md)." >&2
	echo "  \`board-move-guard\` still wants a \`graph-check\` exit 0 naming each id before it moves — this decides" >&2
	echo "  that the column is behind git, never that the work is complete, which is \`released\`'s question:" >&2
	printf '%s\n' "$landed_ids" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2
fi
if [ -n "$abandoned" ]; then
	fail=1
	echo "::error:: claimed-abandoned — In Progress with no ref on main, no PR, no remote branch, idle > ${max_idle}d:" >&2
	for id in $abandoned; do echo "  $id" >&2; done
fi
if [ -n "$unreadable" ]; then
	fail=1
	echo "::error:: unreadable-updatedat — cannot date the claim, so staleness is unknown:" >&2
	for id in $unreadable; do echo "  $id" >&2; done
fi

wip=$(jq -r '[.[] | select(.status == "In Progress")] | length' <<<"$issues")
n_landed=$(grep -c . <<<"$landed_ids" || true)
n_aband=0
for id in $abandoned; do n_aband=$((n_aband + 1)); done
echo "in-progress-drain: $wip In Progress — $n_landed landed-unswept, $n_aband claimed-abandoned"
exit "$fail"
